#N canvas 20 20 4000 2700 12;
#X declare -lib pdlua;
#X text 20 20 m_organelle -- the Organelle's own front panel as a device layer: mother.pd's reserved names in \, named controls out on param \, with no notion of what they do. ref/device/organelle.md, f 58;
#X text 20 170 creation args: none. inlets: none. outlets: none., f 58;
#X obj 20 300 r aux;
#X obj 260 300 r knob1;
#X obj 500 300 r knob2;
#X obj 740 300 r knob3;
#X obj 980 300 r knob4;
#X obj 420 420 select 1 0;
#X obj 260 350 change -1;
#X obj 500 350 change -1;
#X obj 740 350 change -1;
#X obj 980 350 change -1;
#X msg 420 480 modal shift;
#X msg 260 410 og-knob-1 \$1;
#X msg 500 410 og-knob-2 \$1;
#X msg 740 410 og-knob-3 \$1;
#X msg 980 410 og-knob-4 \$1;
#X obj 20 580 s disp;
#X obj 220 580 s param;
#X text 20 660 og- because m_nano already publishes knob-1 to knob-9 \, and one hyphen is not a distinction to trust inside a route box., f 58;
#X text 1200 1950 aux is the keyboard's modifier \, not a control: it publishes no name \, and tells the Lua each edge as shift 1 or shift 0 \, so the keys publish og-shift-60 to og-shift-84. Both edges are used: a modifier you cannot let go of is not one., f 58;
#X text 20 950 A message box needs no list trim: og-knob-1 \$1 already has the control as its selector., f 58;
#X text 640 520 Each of the three sources (aux \, the knobs \, the keyboard) has its own presence outlet \, rightmost \, so seen goes out before the value., f 44;
#X text 640 780 change -1: mother pushes the knobs once at load and then says nothing (item 237) \, which u_map's pickup depends on. A bare change holds 0 \, so a knob parked at 0 would never publish \, and -1 is never a knob value., f 60;
#X text 640 1060 Not here: the encoder \, encbut and the pedal jack. Asking for the encoder takes its click too \, the instrument's way out (items 313 and 314). The pedal jack belongs in this file once it has something to drive., f 44;
#X text 20 1250 The knobs go to param only: a raw 0-to-1 position is no readable row \, so u_map draws the mapped value \, or the raw one when nothing maps it (item 242)., f 62;
#X msg 440 560 seen m_organelle;
#X obj 440 620 s presence;
#X obj 1000 1200 loadbang;
#X msg 1000 1260 expect m_organelle passive;
#X obj 1000 1320 s presence;
#X text 1100 950 Liveness off the fan-in: there is no channel gate and no reply to match \, so anything at all from mother is the evidence. It is weaker than the other devices' \, which the kind says., f 40;
#X text 1000 1400 passive: it registers and publishes seen \, with no c_presence \, so it is never aged or declared lost. mother pushes the knobs once at load (item 237) \, so a last-heard clock would warn a few seconds into every boot (ref/module/presence.md)., f 44;
#X obj 1330 300 r notes;
#X obj 1600 710 s param;
#X text 1600 780 notes is one of mother's own names \, not a C-2 allowlist name: the m_ layer that owns the device reads it. Do not move it into the allowlist., f 62;
#X text 1600 1000 One control per key: param carries one value \, so the pitch rides in the name (og-key-60 to og-key-84 \, item 293) and the velocity is the value. A release is velocity 0 and is published: a note-off is a real event., f 62;
#X text 1600 1560 The keys do not reach disp \, and u_map does not draw them raw: a chord would evict the five-row screen twice per note (item 293 \, ref/module/map.md)., f 62;
#X msg 1980 240 seen m_organelle;
#X obj 1980 300 s presence;
#X obj 260 470 t a b;
#X text 260 1560 The knobs' own fan-in \, so their boot push publishes seen too: it is the only thing mother sends unasked. b before a \, so seen goes first (ref/module/presence.md)., f 44;
#X obj 20 360 t f f f;
#X obj 20 440 s \$0-shift;
#X msg 560 480 modal-off;
#X text 20 1950 The layer is latched per key at press time \, so a release carries the name its press did. Read live \, letting go of aux before the key would send og-key-72 after og-shift-72: a stuck Volca note in mode 1 (ref/device/organelle.md). The latch is the Lua's \, one entry per key \, so a chord's keys keep their own layers., f 84;
#X obj 1330 360 t a b;
#X obj 1330 560 m_organelle-lua;
#X obj 1500 420 r \$0-shift;
#X msg 1500 480 shift \$1;
#X obj 1330 700 s err;
#X obj 2400 300 loadbang;
#X obj 2400 360 t b b;
#X msg 2560 420 init;
#X msg 1660 640 0;
#X obj 2400 700 spigot 1;
#X msg 2400 760 fail m_organelle no-lua;
#X obj 2400 820 s err;
#X obj 3500 300 declare -lib pdlua;
#X text 2400 1020 The keys are Lua: m_organelle-lua names each key og-key-NN or og-shift-NN by the layer aux held when it went down \, and passes the velocity. The rules are m_organelle.lua \, tested without Pd by test/gate/lua/m_organelle_test.lua (ref/device/organelle.md)., f 62;
#X text 2400 1300 At load the Lua answers init with a bang on its last outlet \, which shuts the spigot. No bang means it never loaded -- pdlua missing \, or an error in the Lua -- and fail m_organelle no-lua says so on the screen: the keys are dead \, the knobs and aux are not. A Lua error is fail m_organelle lua-<rule> (C-12)., f 62;
